  "image_name": "02c9fb611ebbb6f0ce80f05253994bc6.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares three silicon contact designs and how charge carriers move through them. In all cases, textured silicon surfaces are coated with thin dielectric layers and contacted by metal, and the arrows distinguish desired from undesired particle flow. Red arrows represent electron flow and green arrows represent hole flow; solid arrows indicate useful transport, while dashed arrows indicate parasitic leakage or recombination paths.\n\nThe left schematic shows a p-type silicon structure with an n+ crystalline silicon region near the top surface. Incoming light is indicated, and the desired current mainly involves electrons moving upward to the front contact and holes moving downward, but some dashed paths show unwanted carrier transfer.\n\nThe middle design switches to n-type silicon with a p+ crystalline silicon layer at the illuminated side and an n+ poly-Si contact at the rear. Electrons and holes are explicitly labeled, emphasizing selective collection of holes at the front and electrons at the back, again with a smaller undesired backflow channel.\n\nThe right schematic returns to p-type silicon but places p+ regions beneath the front contacts and an n+ poly-Si rear contact. Overall, the figure highlights contact selectivity and the combination of pinhole-mediated transport and tunneling in passivating-contact solar-cell architectures.",
